ferring the more right-skewed lottery — but in this case, with only negative and zero outcomes, the 'right' side (positive) has no mass. So the term 'right-skewed' is likely a misnomer here. Nevertheless, based on the provided definition, the reason compares the probability of unfavorable outcomes (losses) and prefers the one with a greater portion of probability on unfavorable outcomes. So, in this problem, B has 25% chance of -3000 (a loss) vs. A’s 20% chance of -4000. Since 25% > 20%, B has more probability mass on unfavorable outcomes. Thus, according to the decision reason, B should be preferred. The information relevant to the decision reason is: probability of loss for A = 20%, for B = 25%, and the loss amounts are -4000 and -3000 respectively. The reason applies to this problem because the outcomes are defined and probabilities are given, and the skewness can be assessed via the distribution of probability on unfavorable outcomes.",
